// 27 entity kinds · 158 distinct field names · 56 rules · 11 relation types · 41 skills.
//
// `docs/product.md` says this is for engineers, designers and product managers. A designer is
// not going to learn `witness-kind`, and the honest reading is that the AGENT holds the
// vocabulary while the humans read output — which is fine, and is not what the doctrine says.
//
// `adopt-the-method` opens by teaching what a repository may never redefine. That is the right
// rule and the wrong first sentence: it answers a question nobody has yet, and the question
// they do have — what is the least I must declare before this thing will check — has no answer
// anywhere. So an adopter does the fast thing instead, which is to copy an existing record and
// mutate it. That is transcription, the one move this method spends its longest argument against,
// and the vocabulary's size is what makes it the rational choice.

thin-slice "TS.260823.09" {
    slug "the-vocabulary-you-need-to-start"
    title "Say what the least is that a repository must declare, before teaching the rest"
    kind "view"
    realizes "CAP.delivery-record"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    read-model "where-to-start"
    content-owned-by "CAP.delivery-record"
    actor "the-adopter"

    trigger "somebody opens the method, counts twenty-seven kinds, and reaches for an existing record to copy"
    outcome "the smallest declarable set is named as such, with every other kind arriving when the work asks for it"

    scenario #"""
        Given a repository under the method and nothing else
        When the adopter asks what they must declare
        Then they get the minimum that will check — and each remaining kind is named against
             the moment that calls for it, rather than presented as a vocabulary to learn
             before starting.
        """#

    step "1" does="the minimum is computed, not chosen. Which kinds a record must hold before `praxis check` passes is a fact about the schema, and a hand-written list of it is a second copy that drifts"
    step "2" does="every other kind names its trigger — the moment in the work that calls for it. A kind with no stated trigger is one an adopter has to guess the use of"
    step "3" does="the answer is a view, composed on demand. A tutorial goes stale against a schema that grows; a projection cannot"
    step "4" does="`adopt-the-method` opens with this and reaches `may never redefine` afterwards, which is the order the questions actually arrive in"

    excludes "reducing the vocabulary. Twenty-seven kinds may well be right for what this method \
        computes, and shrinking it to be teachable would trade a real guarantee for a smaller \
        first page"
    excludes "a tiered or 'lite' profile that checks less. A method a repository can weaken \
        locally reports whatever that repository wanted to hear, and this is that rule restated \
        as a beginner mode"
    excludes "teaching the vocabulary. This says which kinds and when; what each MEANS is what \
        `praxis schema` already prints"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="`adopt-the-method` leads with the minimum and orders the rest by the moment that calls for it"
    layer "docs"        reaches="`what is the least I must declare` is answerable, and the answer is derived from the schema rather than maintained beside it"

    claim "C1" text="the minimum declarable set is derived from the schema, not listed by hand" \
        settled-by="a test adding a required kind to the method and asserting the view includes it with no other change"
    claim "C2" text="a record holding only the minimum checks clean" \
        settled-by="a test building a record from exactly what the view names and asserting `praxis check` passes"
    claim "C3" text="every kind outside the minimum names the moment that calls for it" \
        settled-by="a test asserting a kind with no declared trigger is reported, since an adopter cannot act on it"

    depends-on "TS.260823.04 — an adopter following the minimum has to be able to write each field without knowing the engine, or the shortest path is still to copy"

    useful-alone "the fastest route into this method stops being transcription. Today the honest \
        first instruction is `copy somebody's record and change the words`, which is precisely \
        what `adopt-the-method` spends its longest section arguing nobody should do"

    attacks "S7"

    useful-to "the-adopter"

    cut-from "the validation review of 2026-08-22, which built a first record by following the \
        skills rather than copying, and measured what that cost"

    principle-note "the next agent should not re-derive what is already known. An adopter facing \
        an undifferentiated vocabulary re-derives which parts of it matter — every time, in every \
        repository, and never into the record"

    proves "the smallest set a repository must declare is stated and derived"
    does-not-prove "that the vocabulary is learnable, or that an adopter will succeed with it. \
        It removes the reason to start by copying"
}
